============ ex00 ===========

Exercise 00
ft_putchar
Turn-in directory: ex00/
Files to turn in: ft_putchar.c
Allowed functions: write
• Write a function that displays the character passed as a parameter.
• The function should be prototyped as follows:
  void ft_putchar(char c);
To display the character, you must use the write function as follows:
  write(1, &c, 1);

*************
in C, a character usually use 'A', attention, single quote
char, is a data type, is to save a character
for example:
  char c
    create a thing named c, to save a character
    if c = 'A'

parameter:
  with the example given in ex00, void ft_putchar(char c)
  here the part char c is a parameter
  can understand it as: use char c to run ft_putchar
  here char: type, c: name
  the type of the character: a parameter named as c
  so ft_putchar('A'); can be understood as:
    'A' -> entered as parameter c 
    now what c saved is 'A'
    and ft_putchar can run
char c here is not a value, it's a parameter, it says that the function need to receive a value in type char, and inside this function we call this value as c

definition:
  ft_putchar(char c)
  here char c is the parameter
use:
  ft_putchar('A')
  here 'A' is the argument/value

we can separete the example here in 3 parts
  example in ex00: void ft_putchar(char c);
part 1: void
  void: what to return
part 2: ft_putchar
  ft_putchar: the function name
part 3: (char c)
  (char c): what to receive

void: when the function finished its work, don't return a value
void returns nothing

write itself is a function
write(part1, part2, part3);
example:
write(1, &c, 1);

part1: write it to where -> standard output, which is terminal
  in the example, the first 1 here means file descriptor 1=standard output(stdout)
  0 -> standard input (stdin)
  1 -> standard output (stdout)
  2 -> standard error (stderr)
  stdout and stderr usually show in terminal as well, just two different output streams
part2: where is the data -> the memory address where character c is
part3: how many to write -> 1 byte in the example

what does & mean here?
  c = value
  &c = address of c
why write needs &c?
  write needs to know, where is the data that you wants me to output
  &c means, go to the memory address of c to find the data

'K' -> character -> char -> what we need in this ex00
"K" -> string -> don't need it now

function prototype vs. function definition vs. function body
----------
{
  ...
}
----------
the thing between ---- is the function body

CPU can't execute the C source code directly, since C source code is more readable for human beings, not for the computer
so we need a compiler to make the C source code readable for the computer, turn the C source code to readable machine code
in 42 Moulinette will use cc -Wall -Wextra -Werror to compile my codes
cc: command to use C compiler

1. why use write, we need #include <unistd.h>?
because write is not a function created by me, it's a function offered by the system itself
how a compiler know what write is?
#include <unistd.h>
